NARR = [
"One loose electrode can spoil a whole channel, and the signal alone doesn't always tell you which one. So the ADS1299 can test every electrode for you. The datasheet even points out that although it's called lead-off detection, it's really electrode-off detection.",
"The idea is the same as Ohm's law. Push a tiny, known current through the electrode, and measure the voltage it creates. The voltage equals the current times the impedance. With 6 nanoamps, a good contact of 10 kilo-ohms gives only 60 microvolts. A poor contact of 100 kilo-ohms gives 600 microvolts, and 1 megohm gives 6 millivolts. So the bigger the voltage, the worse the contact.",
"Inside the chip, a small current source sits in front of each input, ahead of the amplifier, like the box called Lead-Off Excitation Source in the block diagram. Two registers, LOFF_SENSP and LOFF_SENSN, choose which inputs get the current, one bit for every P input and every N input. The size of the current is set in the LOFF register: 6 nanoamps, 24 nanoamps, 6 microamps or 24 microamps.",
"The chip offers two methods. The first is DC. A tiny steady current pulls one side of the channel toward the positive supply, and the other side toward the negative supply. If an electrode is connected, the current flows through your skin and the voltage barely moves. If it's off, nothing can carry the current, so the input is pushed all the way to the supply, and the channel saturates. Comparators with adjustable thresholds then tell you which input is off. Their results are stored in the LOFF_STATP and LOFF_STATN registers, and sent along with every sample.",
"The second method is AC, and it's the one the Cyton uses. Instead of a steady current, the chip flips the current back and forth at a fixed frequency, 7.8 or 31.2 hertz. That small tone travels through the amplifier and the converter like any other signal, and shows up in your recorded data. A good electrode gives a tiny tone. A poor electrode gives a big one. Measure the size of the tone at that frequency, and you can calculate the electrode impedance.",
"Why 31.2 hertz? It comes from the chip's own 2.048 megahertz clock, divided by 65,536, so the frequency is precise. It sits inside the band the chip already records, so no extra measuring hardware is needed. And since it's one exact frequency, software can pick it out even while you're recording EEG.",
"On the Cyton, the firmware sets 6 nanoamps at 31.2 hertz when the board starts or resets. A command from the software then switches the current on for one input at a time, using those same LOFF_SENS bits. The OpenBCI GUI measures the 31.2 hertz tone in each channel and turns it into an impedance. Remember that the current also passes through the 2.2 kilo-ohm protection resistor, so every reading includes that small extra.",
"The bias electrode has its own check, but only at power-up. A current source and a comparator test whether the bias electrode is connected. During normal recording this check can't run, because it needs the bias amplifier to be switched off.",
"To sum up. Lead-off detection pushes a tiny known current through each electrode and measures the voltage. DC mode quickly flags an electrode that is off. AC mode gives you an actual impedance number, and that's what the Cyton uses: 6 nanoamps at 31.2 hertz. It's built into the ADS1299, so on your own board it costs no extra parts, only a few register settings in the firmware.",
]
TITLES = ["Why check the electrodes", "The idea: Ohm's law", "Where the current comes from", "Method 1: DC", "Method 2: AC", "Why 31.2 hertz", "On the Cyton", "The bias electrode", "Summary"]
